From 64ff59911b520d078163c232f2d2b42c8a6039b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SorinS <ssuciu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:10:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] --debug implies -O0 unless an -O is given

Goose's inliner folds small functions into their callers, so under a
debugger they have no frame: a breakpoint inside one stops in the caller,
`finish` has nowhere to return to, and its parameters are not shown.
--debug now leaves the inlining level at -O0 unless the command line
names one; an explicit -O1 or -O2 still wins. Folding and propagation
run at every level as before, and so does tail recursion into loops.

Kept apart from the #line patch so either can be taken alone.

Test: on a program whose main calls area() at line 9,

    goose build --debug -o prog prog.goose
    lldb --batch -o 'b prog.goose:2' -o run -o bt ./prog

stops in area_g with its parameters, called from prog.goose:9.
